mission 1 "u1-boundaries" "1" "One responsibility, two C++ files"
requires "001_stable_object_identity"
requires "002_debugging_invariants"
scope "read" "src/main.cpp"
scope "read" "src/UI/AppUISetup.h"
scope "read" "src/UI/AppUISetup.cpp"
step "why" "Explain" "ack" "Your first University session"
why "Your two pilot missions are complete. PandaBank now creates separate objects and checks their IDs in Debug. The next learning topic is how C++ files cooperate. Today, most UI behavior sits in AppUISetup.cpp, but main.cpp can still call it because a small header declares the function. A declaration tells another file what can be called; the definition supplies the behavior. Understanding that existing connection will let you later move one responsibility into a focused header and implementation without rewriting the app. This first University session records your observations only. It does not change PandaBank or certify a refactor."
action "Type next to inspect this existing boundary one step at a time. Use more, syntax or professional whenever you want detail."
expect "The next step asks for one short prediction. You do not need to fill out a Markdown worksheet."
more "Pandata University is a separate executable in this repository. Its engine reads this lesson from a file; its screen code does not contain a special branch for your lesson. Answers and hints are saved to separate progress snapshots. Closing and reopening resumes this step."
professional "Small C++ programs often share declarations through focused headers and compile definitions from .cpp files. Header-only libraries can also be valid when their definitions obey the language rules. File splitting is useful when it clarifies responsibility, not when every tiny function gets a separate file."
syntax "declaration" "A statement describing a name/type/function so another part of the program can use it."
syntax "definition" "The implementation or complete description that gives the declared thing its substance."
end
step "predict" "Predict" "response" "Make one prediction"
why "main.cpp includes AppUISetup.h and calls AppUISetup(clear_color). The header declares that function, while AppUISetup.cpp defines its body. Each .cpp is compiled separately, then their results are linked together. That means reading a declaration and finding executable behavior happen at different stages. You already have a working example in PandaBank, so there is no need to build a new practice application to see the boundary. We can first predict what would fail if one connection were missing, then compare that prediction with the actual project entries. Do not delete anything to test this yet."
action "Type answer followed by one word or sentence: if the header remains but AppUISetup.cpp is omitted from the build, would you expect a compiler problem or a linker problem?"
expect "Your prediction is recorded as a prediction, not automatically judged correct. Then type next."
hint "Ask which stage has already seen the function declaration, and which stage must find the function body."
more "A compile step handles one translation unit. Linking brings compiled results and libraries together. A successful include does not automatically add a .cpp to the build."
professional "An alternative implementation is judged by its declarations, definitions and link behavior, not whether it matches an expected file layout exactly."
syntax "translation unit" "The code produced from one .cpp plus its included headers after preprocessing. It is compiled as one unit, not run as a script."
end
step "inspect" "Inspect" "response" "Find the existing connection"
why "Now inspect the files already working in your project. AppUISetup.h is small enough to see its job immediately: it includes the needed type declaration and declares AppUISetup. AppUISetup.cpp supplies the body. main.cpp includes the header and makes the call inside the frame loop. None of those steps requires copying the function body into main.cpp. Looking at this path before moving code helps you choose a useful boundary later. This console does not have an editor or source reader yet, so use your existing Visual Studio project for the inspection. No build settings or source edits are needed."
action "In Visual Studio, open src/UI/AppUISetup.h and src/UI/AppUISetup.cpp. Type answer followed by the filename that contains the function body."
expect "A short observation is saved. Use next when you have found both the declaration and the definition."
hint "Look for the function's braces and the statements inside them."
more "Do not confuse a declaration ending with a semicolon with a function body. Also inspect the include near the top of main.cpp to trace how its compiler learns the declaration."
syntax "#include" "Makes the included file's declarations/text available during preprocessing; it does not itself tell the build to compile a separate .cpp."
end
step "transfer" "Review" "response" "Choose a future responsibility"
why "Before moving code, choose what the new file would be responsible for. AppUISetup.cpp currently contains object data, drawing, spawning, popup behavior, animation and diagnostics. A useful split gives one part a clear job and a small interface. Splitting random lines into more files would make navigation harder without helping ownership or testing. Your accepted X-macro route does not need replacement simply because code moves between files. For now, name a small responsibility you would want to inspect first. This is a proposal for the next product lesson, not permission for the engine to perform the refactor automatically."
action "Type answer followed by one responsibility you would consider separating, such as a short noun phrase. Do not write or move any code yet."
expect "The proposal is saved for review. It will not be marked as a completed implementation."
professional "Production teams often separate persistent data from rendering so data can be tested and saved without a window. Small prototypes may keep them together temporarily. The right next split depends on the behavior that needs testing, not an arbitrary target folder diagram."
hint "Pick one job from the list rather than a whole replacement architecture."
end
step "checkpoint" "Checkpoint" "ack" "Your observations are saved"
why "This session has recorded your prediction, the file you inspected, and one proposed responsibility. These are useful starting evidence for the next C++ lesson, but they are not a compiler test or proof that a refactor works. University keeps that distinction explicit. The current foundation saves local snapshots automatically, including hint use and previous short responses. Git status, real build actions and an embedded mentor come in later stages. You still own the same PandaBank files in Visual Studio. Ending this session will not edit them, replace your commit history, or silently create a Git commit."
action "Type next to finish this orientation, or quit to resume it later."
expect "The session ends after a saved checkpoint. There is no additional Markdown mission to complete now."
more "Keep course/university/progress with your local backups. The snapshots are plain text. Future repository actions will collect build output and show Git status/diffs; they are not implemented in this slice."
end
finish
